AB  - Transformer architectures have become the dominant backbone for modern natural language processing (NLP), enabling major advances in language understanding, generation, and interactive systems. This review summarizes the methodological foundations of Transformers, organizes key model families (encoder-only, decoder-only, and encoder-decoder), and surveys representative pre-training objectives, adaptation/alignment techniques, and efficiency improvements for long-context and large-scale training. We further provide a task-oriented review of Transformer applications, including machine translation, information extraction, question answering, summarization, dialogue, and information retrieval, with a focused discussion of Chinese NLP research and evaluation resources. Finally, we outline open challenges-data governance, hallucination/factuality, controllability, long-context reliability, and efficient deployment-and highlight promising research directions such as retrieval-augmented generation, parameter-efficient tuning, and multimodal/agentic extensions.
ER  - 

