AB  - Producing long-form scientific writing remains one of the most challenging tasks for large language models (LLMs). Unlike short summaries or answers, full scientific sections and surveys demand thousands of words of coherent, faithful, and well-structured text. In this paper, we introduce SciLong2LongEval, a comprehensive evaluation designed to push LLMs beyond short outputs and test their ability to generate long-to-long scientific text. We evaluate $\mathbf{1 3}$ state-of-the-art LLMs with 10 open-source and 3 closed-source models on tasks ranging from 2,000 -word sections to 10,000 -word surveys, using both automatic and expert-based assessments. Our results reveal clear scaling patterns: small models collapse under long output, and medium-sized LLMs handle only moderate lengths. Although large open-source models begin to rival, they under-perform compared to closed systems. In particular, GPT-4 consistently delivers the most coherent and academically styled text, although emerging large open models such as DeepSeek-70B and Qwen272B show promising improvements. Through quantitative scores and qualitative analysis, including lexical diversity and faithfulness to citations, our study highlights the current limitations of LLMs in scientific writing and provides a rigorous benchmark for future progress.
ER  - 

