AB  - In this paper, we present a comprehensive survey on the robustness and generalization of large language models (LLMs) under adversarial and real-world conditions, covering research published between 2020 and 2025. The survey systematically analyzes adversarial vulnerabilities, including prompt injection attacks and word level perturbations, and examines the extent to which LLMs can generalize effectively across diverse domains and unseen tasks. Evaluation frameworks such as CheckList, TextAttack, and Robustness Gym are thoroughly reviewed, along with adversarial robustness benchmarks including ANLI, AdvGLUE, and datasets produced through the Dynabench platform. Additionally, key metrics used to measure model robustness and generalization—such as adversarial accuracy, robustness degradation, and performance consistency are critically evaluated.The robustness characteristics of state-of-the-art models, including GPT-4, Claude, and LLaMA, are compared and synthesized, highlighting their strengths and vulnerabilities in adversarial scenarios and under distribution shifts. Despite significant advancements, the findings indicate persistent vulnerabilities across all models under targeted adversarial attacks or domain variation. Furthermore, the paper reviews and assesses robustness improvement techniques, including adversarial training, data augmentation strategies, instruction tuning, and constitutional AI, identifying their effectiveness as well as limitations. Ultimately, this survey emphasizes the necessity for comprehensive robustness evaluations that reflect diverse adversarial and practical stressors, underscoring the critical role of robust language models for safe and reliable real-world deployments.
ER  - 

