AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are enabling automation across multiple stages of the integrated circuit design flow. Most existing research applies LLMs at higher levels of abstraction, particularly for register-transfer level (RTL) code generation, testbench creation, and electronic design automation script generation and optimization, leveraging their strong natural language and code modeling capabilities. In contrast, gatelevel netlists (GLNs) remain relatively underexplored from an LLM perspective. GLNs lack high-level semantic constructs, are significantly larger than RTL codes, and are governed by strict structural and logical constraints. However, GLNs can be naturally represented as graphs, and LLM- and transformer-based approaches have been extensively studied for graph reasoning tasks, such as graph completion. In this work, we present a survey of existing LLM-based approaches for GLN applications, propose a taxonomy of current methods, and analyze the applicability of LLM-based graph techniques to GLN tasks.
