AB  - The release of advanced Large Language Models (LLMs) like GPT-4, alongside Arabic-centric architectures such as Jais and ALLaM, has reshaped the digital landscape. While these tools drive innovation, they also threaten information integrity, creating an urgent need for reliable detection systems. This task is particularly challenging in the Arabic context, where models must navigate a complex linguistic environment defined by rich morphology, diglossia (the coexistence of Standard Arabic and dialects), and the stochastic use of diacritics. In this survey, we review the current state of the art in Arabic AI text detection. We introduce a taxonomy that organizes existing research into four primary schools: Stylometric Analysis, which targets the unique “fingerprints” of generative models; Deep Learning approaches utilizing fine-tuned Transformers; Hybrid Architectures; and the newly emerging Ensemble Learning frameworks. Furthermore, we evaluate the resilience of these systems against adversarial attacks and discuss findings from the AraGenEval 2025 shared task. The paper concludes by identifying critical research gaps and outlining a strategic roadmap for the future of robust Arabic AI detection.
