AB  - Work-Life Balance (WLB) is also becoming a concept acknowledged as having a contribution to the retention, efficiency, and well-being of employees. Subjectivity, random sampling, and lack of scalability are the demerits of the conventional assessment techniques, including surveys and self-reporting. To enable the proactive workforce optimisation and retention policies, this research introduces the new data-based system to project the specific level of WLB in real-time when deep learning methods are used. We obtain a multi-modal data that includes anonymised personal information, context-dependent aspects (loaded calendar, activity after-hours, etc.), and objective ones (work logs, frequency of meetings, the pattern of digital communications, time stamps, etc.). Upon the extensive preprocessing and feature engineering, we find and compare several ostensibly deep networks to forecast WLB indicators in a linear format, which are temporal convolutional networks (TCN), attention-based transformers, and recurrent neural networks (LSTM/GRU). A hybrid Transformer-LSTM is our best model that leads to a high predictive accuracy and generalisation in the case of the predictive accuracy and strong generalisation across departments and time. Such critical predictors as the frequency of emails after the working hours, the number of the working days in a row, and the concentration of meetings are highlighted with the help of such model interpretability tools as SHAP and attention visualisation. And, lastly, we also give an implementation example by the deployment prototype to which the HR systems are connected, and under which it is possible to implement dynamic interventions (e.g., workload redistribution, provision of wellness resources) in the cases when the forecasted WLB is not within the healthy limits. The results determine the usefulness of objective and scalable WLB surveillance as an enhancer of organisational well-being and turnover.
ER  - 

