AB  - In today's digital world, recommender systems are crucial for helping users navigate vast online content. They struggle with the cold-start problem for new users/items with little or no interaction data. Cross-domain recommendation (CDR) transfers knowledge from data-rich source domains to sparse target domains, but graph-based CDR models still face poor initialization for extremely cold-start entities. We propose LGCCDR, an LLM-boosted graph contrastive learning framework that (i.) generates dense semantic embeddings for users and items using a sentence transformer (LLM-based), (ii.) constructs a unified u ser-item g raph i nitialized w ith t hose e mbeddings, (iii.) refines e m beddings w i tha Graph Ne ural Ne twork, and aligns cross-domain user representations via contrastive learning. This pipeline is designed to address the cold-start problem by replacing random initialization with semantically meaningful representations and by enforcing cross-domain alignment. As a survey-driven conceptual framework, this work focuses on model design and analysis rather than experimental evaluation. Future work will explore scalability and multi-modal extensions.
ER  - 

