Y1  - 2026
AB  - Design patterns capture reusable solutions to recurring design problems and play a central role in program comprehension, software maintenance, and software evolution. Over the past three decades, numerous approaches have been proposed to automatically detect and recover design patterns from software artifacts. While early research relied on rule-based specifications, structural matching, and metric-driven analysis, the last decade has witnessed a pronounced shift toward machine learning and artificial intelligence–based detection techniques. This paper presents a comprehensive and updated survey of automated design pattern detection approaches, organized using a taxonomy that captures rule-based, graph-based, search-based, machine learning, deep learning, and hybrid techniques. Based on a consolidated corpus of 54 primary detection approaches, the survey introduces a taxonomy that explicitly captures the emergence of machine-learning-based, deep-learning-based, and large language model–based approaches, alongside traditional rule-based and graph-based techniques. A systematic comparative analysis is conducted across detection methodologies, analysis styles, intermediate representations, subject systems, programming languages, design pattern coverage, and evaluation metrics. The survey highlights a clear shift toward data-driven and hybrid design pattern detection frameworks, leveraging feature learning, code embeddings, graph neural networks, transformers, and large language models to improve robustness and scalability. However, challenges remain in benchmark standardization, reliable ground-truth creation, explainability of AI-based models, and large-scale industrial validation.
ER  - 

