AB  - The amount of advances made in automatic text summarization are many. However, much of this work is for high resource languages (e.g., English). Therefore, many low resource languages (i.e., Hindi) have seen very little development with respect to summarization. In particular, the language characteristics of Hindi (e.g., free word order, extensive morphology, limited availability of training data), present significant barriers to developing effective and reliable summarization systems. Research has employed a variety of extraction, abstraction and hybrid approaches, including statistical/graph based techniques as well as neural/transformer based techniques such as BERT, BART, mBART and IndicBART. Despite these advances, the performance of the summarizers is frequently limited due to the lack of training data and/or poorly designed evaluation methodologies. This article provides an overview and analysis of recent developments in Hindi text summarization. Additionally, it outlines three new lines of research: linguistically-informed fine tuning, multi-lingual transformer-based modeling, and the design of Hindi-specific semantic evaluation metrics. Finally, the authors note that the key to developing robust, semantically faithful, Hindi text summarization systems lies in addressing linguistic diversity, evaluation limitations, and adapting systems to specific domains.
ER  - 

