AB  - The massive deployment of the Internet of Things (IoT) has exponentially expanded the cyberattack surface of modern network infrastructures, exposing constrained edge nodes to complex threats. While blockchain-assisted Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) have emerged to preserve data integrity and decentralization, current architectures suffer from systemic limitations in latency, performance overhead, and model opacity. This paper presents an extended comprehensive survey of contemporary blockchain-backed IDS frameworks, establishing a multi-dimensional taxonomy across advanced paradigms including Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), Vision Transformers (ViTs), TinyML, and Large Language Models (LLMs). We detail the practical implementation, real-world applicability, and explicit quantitative methodology governing our systematic PRISMA analysis. Furthermore, we establish explicit mapping between mathematical formulations, algorithmic complexity bounds, and reviewed baseline models. To address conceptual gaps, we propose a scalable DOST-A-MSTCN-ZK-PoA framework and validate its performance through empirical simulation benchmarks against standard state-of-the-art models, demonstrating superior accuracy (98.67%) and significantly lower operational latency (4.2 ms).
