AB  - The ability to computationally interpret human emotion from text is a critical frontier in artificial intelligence, underpinning the development of empathetic and socially aware systems. This paper presents a comprehensive review of the evolution of emotion detection using Natural Language Processing (NLP) and introduces a novel conceptual framework to address key limitations in current models. We systematically survey the landscape of methodologies, beginning with foundational lexiconbased and classical machine learning techniques, progressing through the deep learning era of CNNs and RNNs, and culminating in the state-of-the-art with transformer architectures and Large Language Models (LLMs). The review further provides an essential overview of benchmark datasets and evaluation metrics that drive the field. To push beyond existing capabilities, particularly in capturing nuanced context, we propose a novel conceptual framework integrating a RoBERTa encoder with a specialized emotion-focused attention mechanism. We analyze key applications in mental health, customer experience, and social media, alongside a deep dive into persistent challenges such as sarcasm, multilinguality, and ethical considerations, including the need for Explainable AI (XAI). This work serves as both a detailed survey for researchers and a forward-looking proposal for building the next generation of emotionally intelligent systems.
