AB  - Large language models (LLMs) unite with multiagent systems (MAS) to create agentic AI systems which demonstrate autonomous behavior, persistent memory, advanced planning capabilities and collaborative tool usage. This research evaluates the essential elements required to support large-scale deployment of multi-agent LLM systems which need to be both dependable and maintainable as well as subject to governance. The paper follows the development from traditional MAS systems based on coordination, negotiation and formal protocols to current LLM-based agents which demonstrate collective intelligent behavior. We describe how LLM-based agent orchestration generates new infrastructure needs, which include diverse computing resources, standardized networks, adaptive memory systems, monitoring capabilities, and protection mechanisms. The research evaluates open-source platforms through architectural comparisons while identifying major scalability, sustainability and governance challenges. The survey creates infrastructure maps between compute resources, communication systems, storage needs and policy frameworks to direct future research and engineering development for next-generation agentic AI systems.
