AB  - The rapid advancements in artificial intelligence (AI) have intensified competition between technology firms and national innovation ecosystems, particularly in Large Language Models (LLMs). In early 2025, DeepSeek-R1 emerged as a disruptive force, challenging the established dominance of OpenAI’s ChatGPT. With its low-cost, open-access approach, DeepSeek-R1 signals a shift in AI development, fostering new discussions on cost efficiency, open- source innovation, and performance benchmarking. This paper presents a comparative analytical survey of the two most influential LLM ecosystems DeepSeek and OpenAI, analyzing their based models and respective contributions to AI research, economic influence, and geopolitical implications. The analysis indicates that alternative training methods can reduce computational costs while maintaining competitive performance on established benchmarks. Beyond technical considerations and by comparing key attributes such as accessibility, technological advancements, and strategic positioning, this paper highlights how AI innovation is not only shaping technological competition and future governance, but also the digital sovereignty of AI systems.
