AB  - The use of artificial intelligence in legal field has become more popular, leading to a rise of interest in large language models (LLMs) to perform judgment prediction, document summarization, information retrieval, and legal reasoning, among other tasks. As research in this area continues to expand, it has become important to clearly understand which models are being used, how they are applied in practice, and how well they perform. This review examines existing studies in legal natural language processing to identify the main modeling approaches explored, the different ways these models are used across legal tasks, and the performance trends reported in prior work. It compares traditional extractive and domain-specific approaches with more recent transformer-based and LLM-driven methods, highlighting both consistent improvements and remaining limitations. Overall, this paper offers a clear overview of the current legal AI landscape and serves as a reference for understanding how different modeling approaches have been evaluated. Based on these insights, the paper also points toward a simple dual-mode approach that presents legal outcomes in plain language for citizens and in a structured FIRAC format for students and legal professionals.
