AB  - Arabic is spoken by more than 400 million people worldwide; however, its linguistic characteristics, including rich morphology and dialectal variation, pose significant challenges for natural language processing. Consequently, developing effective Arabic Islamic question answering (QA) systems remains difficult, particularly due to the linguistic complexity of Arabic and the limited availability of high-quality domain-specific datasets. In the Islamic domain, QA systems are increasingly important for facilitating access to religious sources. This paper presents a systematic survey of Arabic Islamic QA systems, analyzing existing approaches, datasets, and evaluation metrics. The review follows a structured literature selection process based on predefined criteria and PRISMA guidelines to identify relevant studies published between 2014 and 2025. The analyzed studies are categorized into traditional information retrieval approaches, pretrained language models (PLMs), large language models (LLMs) and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) frameworks. The comparative analysis shows a clear shift from keyword-based methods to transformer-based and LLM-driven architectures, which demonstrate improved performance on Quranic QA benchmarks, with reported results reaching F1-scores up to 95% and pAP@10 values of 0.637. Based on these findings, the paper highlights key challenges and recommends future research directions, including the development of robust Arabic Islamic QA datasets, instruction-tuned Arabic LLMs, and RAG-based architectures to improve factual grounding and reduce hallucinations.
ER  - 

