AB  - The rapid growth of large AI models such as large language models (LLMs), vision transformers (ViTs), and multimodal models has created a critical need for efficient inference. This survey reviews a broad spectrum of inference acceleration techniques from algorithmic and architectural methods to model compression, hardware optimizations, and system-level strategies. We discuss dynamic inference methods such as early exiting, mixture-of-experts, and adaptive attention; model compression including pruning, quantization, and weight sharing; hardwareaware optimizations including inference compilers and custom kernels; and system strategies including batching, caching, and scheduling. We compare techniques in terms of latency, throughput, memory, and accuracy trade-offs, providing useful details for practitioners. Notably, methods like aggressive quantization can yield approximately three to four times throughput improvements, knowledge distillation can speed up inference by approximately two times, and advanced schedulers can boost multi-query serving throughput by approximately two to five times. This paper serves as a reference for practitioners and researchers seeking a unified view of inference acceleration techniques.
ER  - 

