AB  - Cyberattacks are becoming more common on the rapidly growing internet. Attackers often use fake or malicious website URLs to trick people to share their confidential information or money. Since attackers now use advanced tricks - such as hiding the real link, redirecting users to another page, or faking a trusted website (domain spoofing), traditional methods like relying on a basic blacklist are no longer enough to stop them. This review paper looks at how we've improved the technology for finding these dangerous URLs. We started with basic machine-learning programs and have moved on to advanced systems using deep learning, combinations of different models (hybrid), methods based on breaking down the URL into code (embedding-based), and models like the ones used in modern language AI (transformer-driven). We looked closely at eleven important studies and compared what kind of information they used to spot a fake URL (like the words in the link, the content of the page, or the server information), the types of models they used, the data they tested with, and how accurate they were. The results show that the most accurate methods are those that use deep learning, hybrid models (like combining CNN and LSTM), and embedding-based methods, especially those that use large language models. These methods are much better at finding complex attack patterns than older techniques. This review points out our current successes, the main obstacles we still face, and where we need to go next to build malicious URL detection systems that are faster, more accurate, and more trustworthy.
ER  - 

