AB  - This paper examines the evolution of multimodal interfaces in Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) with a particular focus on vision-based technologies in intelligent environments that incorporate contextually varying means of interaction, including gesture and face recognition, gaze tracking, and body pose detection. While this area is rapidly evolving, there is still a significant body of work that lacks the components of context-awareness, multimodal fusion, and the intersections of cybersecurity with Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Ambient Computing (AC) that are significant. The primary objective of this paper is to bridge this divide by providing the first in-depth classification of vision-based multimodal interfaces, as an aid to smart environments context-aware system developers. From this study, 150 articles published between 2015 and 2025 were analyzed. The study focuses on the following variables: sensor types (RGB, depth, and thermal), types of fusion (early, late, and hybrid), types of learning (CNNs, vision transformers, and multimodal large language models), types of deployment (edge, cloud, and federated), and types of security in systems (privacy-preserving, adversarial robust, secure authenticated learning). The data shows 68% of the systems use some type of deep learning for multimodal fusion, 42% use edge computing for low processing latency (under 50 ms), and 27% of the systems use a method to deal with the issue of cybersecurity threats or data leaks. A layered taxonomy encompassing perception, cognition, interaction, and security is suggested to enhance the reliability of smart environments, highlighting the necessity for privacy-aware multimodal frameworks, rigorous model validation, and data governance to safeguard contextual digital assets. This research serves as a pivotal reference for practitioners and researchers, merging AI-driven multimodal HCI frameworks with cybersecurity approaches to foster the creation of adaptable, resilient, and trustworthy smart environments. The developed taxonomy outlines critical elements that facilitate the development of secure, scalable, and human-centered intelligent interaction systems.
ER  - 

